Document three new mcs51 peephole optimizer features in the SDCC manual
(doc/sdccman.lyx, section "The Peephole Optimizer").

  * operandOffset - a new peephole CONDITION function. Added to the list of
    currently-implemented conditions and given a short description: it is
    true when two symbolic operands share a base symbol and differ by a
    fixed byte offset (%2 == %1 + N). The offset N is written first as a
    quoted literal, followed by the two operands.
  * tail    - a new rule MODIFIER. Scans the whole block without replacing,
    remembers the last match, and replaces only there so rules with
    overlapping windows converge bottom-up. May be combined with restart.
  * retrack - a new rule MODIFIER. After the replacement, asks the port to
    re-run its register/value tracker over the replaced region via an
    optional port hook; ports without the hook ignore the flag.

Both modifiers are added to the rule grammar next to restart. Only the
peephole documentation is touched; no other manual text is changed.

--- doc/sdccman.lyx	2026-03-11 09:20:48.000000000 +0100
+++ doc/sdccman.lyx	2026-03-12 17:00:00.000000000 +0100
@@ -78921,7 +78921,7 @@
 \begin_layout Verse
 
 \family typewriter
-rule := replace [ restart ] '{' <assembly sequence> '
+rule := replace [ restart ] [ tail ] [ retrack ] '{' <assembly sequence> '
 \backslash
 n'
 \begin_inset Newline newline
@@ -79251,6 +79251,26 @@
 \end_layout
 
 \begin_layout Standard
+A rule can also be preceded by the 'tail' modifier,
+ written between the 'replace' keyword and the opening brace just like 'restart'.
+ A normal rule is applied at the first match found while scanning the block from the top;
+ a 'tail' rule instead scans the whole block without replacing,
+ remembers the last position at which the pattern matched,
+ and performs the replacement only there.
+ This lets a rule whose match windows overlap converge from the bottom up,
+ and the 'tail' modifier can be combined with 'restart'.
+\end_layout
+
+\begin_layout Standard
+The 'retrack' modifier,
+ also written between the 'replace' keyword and the opening brace,
+ asks the port to re-run its register and value tracker over the replaced region after the replacement has been made,
+ so that the port can then find further simplifications.
+ It relies on an optional port hook;
+ ports that do not implement the hook ignore the flag.
+\end_layout
+
+\begin_layout Standard
 A conditional function can be attached to a rule.
  Attaching rules are somewhat more involved,
  let's illustrate this with an example.
@@ -79360,7 +79380,8 @@
  operandsLiteral,
  labelIsUncondJump,
  deadMove,
- useAcallAjmp
+ useAcallAjmp,
+ operandOffset
 \emph default
  and 
 \emph on
@@ -79368,6 +79389,16 @@
 \end_layout
 
 \begin_layout Standard
+The 'operandOffset' condition tests whether two symbolic operands refer to the same base symbol with addresses that differ by a fixed number of bytes.
+ It is written operandOffset('N' %1 %2) and is true when %2 equals %1 + N,
+ which is useful for recognizing sequential memory accesses,
+ for example a second 'mov dptr,#(_sym+1)' following 'mov dptr,#_sym'.
+ Because the condition arguments are collected in last-in first-out order,
+ the byte offset N is written first as a quoted literal,
+ followed by the two operands.
+\end_layout
+
+\begin_layout Standard
